    "body": "View of the Moma open pit in Mozambique. Credit: Kenmare Resources.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 23, 2026\nKenmare Resources (LSE, ISE: KMR) shares jumped after the Dublin-based miner agreed in principle to be acquired by Abu Dhabi’s International Resources Holding (IRH) in a deal potentially valued at £352.8 million ($461.6 million).\nIRH’s proposed 380p a share cash offer is 52% above Friday’s London close of 250p for Kenmare stock. It would give Kenmare an enterprise value of £487.1 million, including net debt, according to a statement dated Monday. IRH is also discussing up to $25 million in interim funding for Kenmare.\nKenmare’s directors were inclined to recommend an offer unanimously if the parties settled the remaining terms, according to the statement. No firm offer has been announced.\n“The Kenmare board also considers IRH would be an appropriate owner of Kenmare for the next stage of its growth and development,” Kenmare said in Monday’s statement.\nKenmare shares jumped 20% to 299p Monday afternoon in London, valuing the company at about £339 million. They’ve traded between 247p and 338.88p in the past year.\nDividend halt\nWeak mineral sands prices have squeezed Kenmare’s cash flow and prompted it to suspend dividends. Without interim funding from the bidder, the company expects it would need to raise at least $50 million in equity before expenses in early 2027. It’s already discussing changes to its revolving credit agreement with lenders.\nKenmare’s key asset is the Moma titanium minerals mine in Mozambique.\nPreconditions to the transaction include Mozambican change-of-control consents and completion of outstanding administrative steps for an addendum to Moma’s implementation agreement with the government.\nIRH must also receive confirmation of any Mozambican non-resident capital gains tax liability arising from the acquisition and be satisfied with the amount. Other requirements include shareholder commitments and definitive transaction documents. The conditions may be waived by the bidder, which has until 5 p.m. Dublin time on Dec. 15 to announce a firm offer or withdraw, unless the Irish Takeover Panel extends the deadline.\nAfrican portfolio\nAt Moma, Kenmare produced 233,600 tonnes of ilmenite in the quarter to Sept. 30, 12% more than a year earlier. Delays commissioning Wet Concentrator Plant A nevertheless led Kenmare to cut its 2026 forecast on Oct. 15 to 710,000–750,000 tonnes from about 800,000 tonnes. Ilmenite supplies titanium dioxide used in white pigment.\nAcquiring Kenmare would expand the bidder’s existing African mining portfolio into mineral sands. IRH, a subsidiary of Abu Dhabi-listed Two Point Zero Group, already holds majority interests in Mopani Copper Mines and Alphamin Resources.\nMoma supplies about 6% of global titanium feedstocks, according to Kenmare, and also produces zircon. Kenmare expects Plant A to consistently achieve its nameplate capacity in the first half of 2027, making the commissioning programme a key operating milestone.\nKenmare is also studying whether to recover more value from Moma’s monazite, a mineral containing rare earth elements. It completed a prefeasibility study in the third quarter and is advancing to a definitive feasibility study alongside discussions with potential buyers.\nPrint\nTAG: Africa mining M&A rare earth elements rare earths titanium\nCOMMODITY: Rare Earths Specialty Minerals\nREGION: Africa Europe\nCOMPANY: Kenmare Resources\nRepublish this article",
